.TH CCOL_SELECT_VA 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_select_va, ccol_select_timed_va \- wait for readiness on an inline list of selectables (convenience macro)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_select_va(ready_index, ...);
ccol_select_timed_va(ready_index, timeout_us, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
ccol_select_va() builds a ccol_selectable array from its variadic
arguments. It computes the count of the elements at compile time. It
then passes everything to ccol_select().

ccol_select_timed_va() does the same, but it calls
ccol_select_timed() with the timeout_us that you give. That value is
in microseconds. UINT64_MAX means an infinite wait, 0 means a poll, and
any other value is a deadline.

Each variadic argument must be a ccol_selectable value. Such a value
usually comes from ccol_selectable_from_circq(),
ccol_selectable_from_dynq(), ccol_selectable_from_chan() or
ccol_selectable_from_fd(), with a dir of ccol_select_read or
ccol_select_write. Each macro evaluates every argument exactly one
time.

Neither macro does the receive or the send itself. On ccol_success the
caller must make that call at once. For a queue selectable that wins,
call ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc(). For an fd selectable
that wins, call read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) on its
descriptor.

Each macro expands to a statement expression of GCC and Clang. Each
one gives a value of type ccol_retval_t. You can therefore use them in
an assignment and in a condition.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ready_index  A size_t * that gets the index of the selectable that is
             ready. That index starts at 0. It is valid only when the
             macro returns ccol_success.
timeout_us   Microseconds to wait. UINT64_MAX means an infinite wait.
             This parameter belongs to ccol_select_timed_va() only.
\&...          One or more ccol_selectable values. You must give at
             least one.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            A selectable is ready, and the macro set
                        *ready_index. The caller then does its own
                        explicit receive or send.
ccol_timed_out          The deadline passed and no selectable became
                        ready. This applies to ccol_select_timed_va()
                        only.
ccol_invalid_args       An argument is NULL or zero. Or a selectable
                        holds a NULL queue pointer, or a type tag that
                        the library does not know.
ccol_not_enough_memory  A waiter node failed to allocate. There is one
                        node for each selectable.
ccol_unexpected_failure The setup of epoll or of eventfd failed. An fd
                        selectable that names a regular file or a
                        directory always gives this code, because
                        epoll(7) cannot watch such a file.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The array of the selectables is an ordinary local array on the stack.
There is no heap allocation beyond the one that ccol_select_timed()
already makes.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Each macro uses a statement expression of GCC and Clang, which is
__extension__({ ... }). That is not valid under strict ISO C, which is
-pedantic. It compiles clean with -Wall -Wextra -Werror on both GCC
and Clang, when the build does not pass -pedantic.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
size_t idx;

/* Two circular queues and a ccol_channel, all read-wait: */
ccol_retval_t r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    printf("ready selectable: %zu\en", idx);
    /* claim the message from whichever selectable won, for example: */
    c_message_t msg;
    if (idx == 0 && ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(q0, &msg) == ccol_success)
        free(msg.data);
}

/* Mixed: write-wait on q0, read-wait on ch: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
    ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read));

/* Timed wait with 200 ms deadline: */
r = ccol_select_timed_va(&idx, 200000,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_timed_out) {
    puts("no source ready within 200 ms");
}
.fi
